In `dpll_int_repr`, the tested value is `val = pl_true_int_repr(c, model)`. Reading `pl_true_int_repr` shows it can return `True`, `None`, or `False`: it returns `True` if some literal in the clause is satisfied, sets the result to `None` if it encounters an unassigned literal and no satisfied literal, and otherwise returns `False` when all literals are assigned unsatisfied. Since the loop in `dpll_int_repr` is written to handle all three outcomes (`False` returns `False`, non-`True` clauses are collected as unknown, and no unknown clauses returns the model), the property `val is False` cannot hold for every evaluation of that line.

